      "math_academy_id": "q-11715",
      "knowledge_point_id": "4791998a-2e12-50fa-8127-8fe3482a030d",
      "knowledge_point": "Expressing the Maclaurin Series of a Function in Sigma Notation",
      "problem": "Express the Maclaurin series expansion for $\\frac{1}{1-x},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{1-x}=1+x+{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}+⋯\\,,\\,x\\in (-1,1)\n$$\n\nusing sigma notation.",
      "worked_solution": "The first four terms of the series are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =1 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =x \\\\ {a}_{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can express these terms using a common format, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & ={x}^{0} \\\\ {a}_{1} & ={x}^{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe deduce that the general formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{1-x} & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{x}^{n}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
