    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-125336",
      "knowledge_point_id": "4791998a-2e12-50fa-8127-8fe3482a030d",
      "knowledge_point": "Expressing the Maclaurin Series of a Function in Sigma Notation",
      "problem": "Express the Maclaurin series expansion for ${sin}^{2}⁡x,$ given by\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡x={x}^{2}-\\frac{{2}^{3}{x}^{4}}{4!}+\\frac{{2}^{5}{x}^{6}}{6!}-\\frac{{2}^{7}{x}^{8}}{8!}+⋯\n$$\n\nusing sigma notation.",
      "worked_solution": "The first four terms of the series are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {a}_{2} & =-\\frac{{2}^{3}{x}^{4}}{4!} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{{2}^{5}{x}^{6}}{6!} \\\\ {a}_{4} & =-\\frac{{2}^{7}{x}^{8}}{8!}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can express these terms using a common format, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =\\frac{(-1)^{1+1}{2}^{2\\cdot 1-1}{x}^{2\\cdot 1}}{(2\\cdot 1)!} \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{(-1)^{2+1}{2}^{2\\cdot 2-1}{x}^{2\\cdot 2}}{(2\\cdot 2)!} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{(-1)^{3+1}{2}^{2\\cdot 3-1}{x}^{2\\cdot 3}}{(2\\cdot 3)!} \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{(-1)^{4+1}{2}^{2\\cdot 4-1}{x}^{2\\cdot 4}}{(2\\cdot 4)!}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe deduce that the general formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{2}^{2n-1}{x}^{2n}}{(2n)!}\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡x & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{2}^{2n-1}{x}^{2n}}{(2n)!}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
